the religious life a while,

00:34:45.616 --> 00:34:49.554
you start to realize that all
your attachments to things

00:34:49.554 --> 00:34:53.458
and to people are
pathways to God.

00:34:53.458 --> 00:34:57.495
You know, they're not, they're
symbolic of the love of God.

00:34:57.495 --> 00:34:58.896 align:start size:84% position:16%
Your love for your
father is love of God.

00:34:58.896 --> 00:35:00.098
Your love for your mother

00:35:00.098 --> 00:35:01.332
is the love of the
feminine side of God.

00:35:01.332 --> 00:35:02.967
All these things that
you're looking for,

00:35:02.967 --> 00:35:06.471
that people fail you in,
are because they cannot be

00:35:06.471 --> 00:35:09.073
the perfect thing you're
looking for beyond them.

00:35:09.073 --> 00:35:10.374
And once you attach yourself

00:35:10.374 --> 00:35:11.676 align:start size:81% position:19%
to that perfect
thing beyond them,

00:35:11.676 --> 00:35:13.311 align:start size:81% position:19%
it becomes so much
easier to love

00:35:13.311 --> 00:35:15.613 align:start size:84% position:16%
the imperfect people
with their flaws

00:35:15.613 --> 00:35:18.049
and forgive them for
the ways they can't be

00:35:18.049 --> 00:35:20.017
with they can never be.

00:35:20.017 --> 00:35:23.454
And so, you know, guys
like Fauci are there

00:35:23.454 --> 00:35:25.623
because people are just
desperate for that thing,

00:35:25.623 --> 00:35:27.625
and no one will give
it to, and by the way,

00:35:27.625 --> 00:35:30.361
when I talk about this, when
I talk about being religious,

00:35:30.361 --> 00:35:33.131
I think our churches
have failed us utterly.

00:35:33.131 --> 00:35:36.000 align:start position:0%
You know, almost all of our
churches have failed us utterly.

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:37.468
So don't think that I'm saying,

00:35:37.468 --> 00:35:38.903
"Oh yeah, you gotta go down
and follow that priest."

00:35:38.903 --> 00:35:40.771
I mean, personally,
I think we should,

00:35:40.771 --> 00:35:44.041
I'm lucky I found a great
new church that I love.

00:35:44.041 --> 00:35:45.943 align:start size:84% position:16%
But personally, I
think a lot of people

00:35:45.943 --> 00:35:48.312 align:start size:97% position:3%
should just start to gather
together in their homes

00:35:48.312 --> 00:35:49.847
and start from the beginning

00:35:49.847 --> 00:35:52.316
because our churches have
really abandoned the plot.

00:35:52.316 --> 00:35:54.552
- So linking some of
this stuff together,

00:35:54.552 --> 00:35:55.753 align:start size:84% position:16%
I just spoke a
couple of weeks ago

00:35:55.753 --> 00:35:57.321 align:start size:88% position:13%
at the National
Conservatism Conference

00:35:57.321 --> 00:35:58.990 align:start size:75% position:25%
and Thiel spoke-
- Yeah.

00:35:58.990 --> 00:36:00.458
and Ted Cruz and J.D. Vance.

00:36:00.458 --> 00:36:03.661
It was a really great,
sort of powerhouse event.

00:36:03.661 --> 00:36:06.631 align:start position:0%
And what they were really
trying to do there is figure out

00:36:06.631 --> 00:36:09.267
how conservatism, in many ways,

00:36:09.267 --> 00:36:11.636 align:start size:97% position:3%
can link back to all the
stuff we're talking about here

00:36:11.636 --> 00:36:13.471
and link back to belief,

00:36:13.471 --> 00:36:16.674
but then will there be
room for the outsiders,

00:36:16.674 --> 00:36:19.810 align:start position:0%
for the people that are not
religious or for the gay people?

00:36:19.810 --> 00:36:22.880
And there was a very, I think
the panel of the entire thing

00:36:22.880 --> 00:36:25.816
was me and Douglas Murray,
we both happened to be gay,

00:36:25.816 --> 00:36:27.051
speaking with Sohrab Ahmari,

00:36:27.051 --> 00:36:28.819 align:start size:81% position:19%
who of course,
I'm sure you know,

00:36:28.819 --> 00:36:31.155 align:start size:97% position:3%
Catholic thinker and author-
- Yeah.

00:36:31.155 --> 00:36:34.292 align:start size:84% position:16%
- and Yoram Hazony,
Orthodox Jew,

00:36:34.292 --> 00:36:36.427
trying to figure out,
can this thing fit?

00:36:36.427 --> 00:36:38.362
Can we fit this thing together?

00:36:38.362 --> 00:36:39.931
Now, I'm curious because
you're talking about faith

00:36:39.931 --> 00:36:41.666 align:start size:84% position:16%
and all of this stuff,
your son, Spencer,

00:36:41.666 --> 00:36:42.934
who's a good friend of mine,

00:36:42.934 --> 00:36:44.235
who I think is like
one of the all-stars

00:36:44.235 --> 00:36:46.571 align:start size:84% position:16%
of like the future
conservative movement-

00:36:46.571 --> 00:36:49.106 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Absolutely.
- he happens to be gay,

00:36:49.106 --> 00:36:51.642
it does not matter to me,

00:36:51.642 --> 00:36:53.144 align:start size:84% position:16%
but as you're
talking about this,

00:36:53.144 --> 00:36:56.881
as a father and then as
a political communicator,

00:36:56.881 --> 00:36:59.250
how do you piece all
of this stuff together?

00:36:59.250 --> 00:37:01.919 align:start size:97% position:3%
I know you guys have discussed
it publicly once or twice-

00:37:01.919 --> 00:37:05.656 align:start position:0%
- Yeah, yeah, no, and my opinion
of this has never changed.

00:37:05.656 --> 00:37:06.924
(Andrew laughs)

00:37:06.924 --> 00:37:08.492 align:start size:84% position:16%
My father explained
this to me once

00:37:08.492 --> 00:37:10.194
when we went to a party
when I was a little kid

00:37:10.194 --> 00:37:12.430
and I saw a gay couple
and he explained it to me

00:37:12.430 --> 00:37:13.698
and in those days,

00:37:13.698 --> 00:37:15.800
saying that this was a
psychological disorder

00:37:15.800 --> 00:37:17.235
was the liberal point of view.

00:37:17.235 --> 00:37:18.436 align:start size:84% position:16%
That was the accepting
point of view.

00:37:18.436 --> 00:37:19.637
And that's what he said to me.

00:37:19.637 --> 00:37:21.239 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I remember
shrugging, saying,

00:37:21.239 --> 00:37:22.173
"They're not hurting anybody."

00:37:22.173 --> 00:37:23.407
(Dave and Andrew laughs)

00:37:23.407 --> 00:37:27.044
And I still feel that
way, but I actually,

00:37:27.044 --> 00:37:31.282
I feel that God is
a lot more hilarious

00:37:31.282 --> 00:37:33.017
and creative than
we think he is.

00:37:33.017 --> 00:37:37.221 align:start size:84% position:16%
And a lot of the kind
of pinched, mean,

00:37:37.221 --> 00:37:39.924
you know, like you have to
do what I tell you to do,

00:37:39.924 --> 00:37:42.059
is not really in the gospels.

00:37:42.059 --> 00:37:45.196 align:start size:97% position:3%
And I know people get so
ticked at me when I say this,

00:37:45.196 --> 00:37:46.664
but it really isn't.

00:37:46.664 --> 00:37:50.935 align:start position:0%
And there's a much greater sense
of like abundance of beauty

00:37:50.935 --> 00:37:52.570
and abundance of creation.

00:37:52.570 --> 00:37:54.872
I think we need, it is,

00:37:54.872 --> 00:37:59.577
it helps political people
to talk about a vertical

00:37:59.577 --> 00:38:00.678
world of good and evil.

00:38:01.879 --> 00:38:03.748
But in fact, there
is good and evil.

00:38:03.748 --> 00:38:05.016 align:start size:84% position:16%
There is such a thing
as good and evil,

00:38:05.016 --> 00:38:06.484
and there is that vertical axis.

00:38:06.484 --> 00:38:08.753
But there's also
a horizontal axis

00:38:08.753 --> 00:38:12.723
of centrality and outwardness.

00:38:12.723 --> 00:38:14.892 align:start size:84% position:16%
And I, for instance,
I'm an artist.

00:38:14.892 --> 00:38:17.361
I've lived my life as
a novelist and artist.

00:38:17.361 --> 00:38:18.763
I'm not at the center.

00:38:18.763 --> 00:38:20.898 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, if your a
garbage man disappeared,

00:38:20.898 --> 00:38:21.899
you would miss him

00:38:21.899 --> 00:38:22.767
way, way, way
(Dave laughs)

00:38:22.767 --> 00:38:23.968
before you missed me.

00:38:23.968 --> 00:38:25.436
I'm here to serve him.

00:38:25.436 --> 00:38:26.871 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm here to entertain
him, to beautify his life,

00:38:26.871 --> 00:38:29.173
to beautify the life
of ordinary people

00:38:29.173 --> 00:38:31.075
or central people,
I should call them,

00:38:31.075 --> 00:38:34.045 align:start size:97% position:3%
'cause they're not ordinary,
but central people.

00:38:34.045 --> 00:38:36.113
And I feel this way
about sexuality too.

00:38:36.113 --> 00:38:37.315
I think the center of human life

00:38:37.315 --> 00:38:39.016 align:start size:84% position:16%
is a man and a woman
having children.

00:38:39.016 --> 00:38:41.485 align:start size:88% position:13%
I think if without that,
you know, forgive me,

00:38:41.485 --> 00:38:44.088
but without that, you
don't have a world.

00:38:44.088 --> 00:38:48.125 align:start size:94% position:6%
You don't have that
central kind of polarity.

00:38:48.125 --> 00:38:49.527
- Yeah, I get it.

00:38:49.527 --> 00:38:50.594 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't disagree.
- That human beings, yeah.

00:38:50.594 --> 00:38:52.063 align:start size:84% position:16%
- I don't disagree.
- Right.

00:38:52.063 --> 00:38:53.331 align:start size:94% position:6%
But just because something
is not at that center,

00:38:53.331 --> 00:38:54.999
doesn't mean it's not beautiful.

00:38:54.999 --> 00:38:56.901 align:start size:88% position:13%
And it doesn't mean it's
not part of creation.

00:38:56.901 --> 00:38:58.769 align:start size:97% position:3%
And this is one of the things,
I mean, you look around.

00:38:58.769 --> 00:39:00.204
One of the things
about Spencer's,

00:39:00.204 --> 00:39:02.239 align:start size:97% position:3%
I knew when he was three
years old, I started to think,

00:39:02.239 --> 00:39:04.875
"Ah, this kid could
very well be gay,

00:39:04.875 --> 00:39:08.879 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I'm going to
teach him to be a man

00:39:08.879 --> 00:39:10.448
because I think he
should be a man."

00:39:10.448 --> 00:39:11.849
And I hope I did,

00:39:11.849 --> 00:39:13.517 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I think I actually
believe he's a wonderful man.

00:39:13.517 --> 00:39:16.754 align:start size:88% position:13%
He's a terrific man,
but that was who he was,

00:39:16.754 --> 00:39:19.357
and that was clearly who
he had been born to be.

00:39:19.357 --> 00:39:21.826 align:start size:88% position:13%
And so I think that like
a little bit of just,

00:39:21.826 --> 00:39:23.894
you know, I really do believe

00:39:23.894 --> 00:39:25.863
that Jesus wants us
to blow the hinges

00:39:25.863 --> 00:39:27.365
off the doors of our hearts.

00:39:27.365 --> 00:39:30.301
You know, just like, okay,
are you hurting anybody?

00:39:30.301 --> 00:39:32.236
Are you preaching evil?
Are you're polluting us?

00:39:32.236 --> 00:39:33.738
Are you corrupting us?

00:39:33.738 --> 00:39:36.941
Or are you living in love and
going forward with whatever,

00:39:36.941 --> 00:39:39.276 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, you have
problems, I have problems,

00:39:39.276 --> 00:39:40.978
all God's children got problems.

00:39:40.978 --> 00:39:43.681
You know, that's really
the thing that unites us.

00:39:43.681 --> 00:39:46.350
And so I think if we could
remember as conservatives

00:39:46.350 --> 00:39:49.153 align:start size:81% position:19%
that there is this
horizontal axis

00:39:49.153 --> 00:39:52.256 align:start size:84% position:16%
and we have a
right to prioritize

00:39:53.691 --> 00:39:56.060
mommy and daddy families,
we have a right to say,

00:39:56.060 --> 00:39:57.895 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Yes, that's what's at
the center, we need them,"

00:39:57.895 --> 00:40:00.965
just like we have a right to
prioritize working people,

00:40:00.965 --> 00:40:03.300 align:start size:84% position:16%
people who do certain
things, doctors,

00:40:03.300 --> 00:40:05.002 align:start size:94% position:6%
people who do certain
things that are essential

00:40:05.002 --> 00:40:06.504
to human life.

00:40:06.504 --> 00:40:08.372
But that does not mean we
should not live in love

00:40:08.372 --> 00:40:11.609
and appreciate the beauty
of other kinds of things

00:40:11.609 --> 00:40:14.311
that are not central, like art.

00:40:14.311 --> 00:40:18.716 align:start size:97% position:3%
Again, you know, if the
great violinist disappears,

00:40:18.716 --> 00:40:21.118
you're gonna miss
your carpenter.

00:40:21.118 --> 00:40:23.954
You're gonna miss the lady
who was cleaning your house

00:40:23.954 --> 00:40:25.456
before you miss the
great violinist.

00:40:25.456 --> 00:40:27.224
He's not at the center of
things, but he's beautiful.

00:40:27.224 --> 00:40:29.493
He makes life worth
living, you know?

00:40:29.493 --> 00:40:33.898 align:start position:0%
And I just, that is the
attitude that I really do have.

00:40:33.898 --> 00:40:36.033 align:start size:97% position:3%
That's not a put on attitude,
it's not a philosophy.

00:40:36.033 --> 00:40:37.568
That's the way I actually feel.

00:40:37.568 --> 00:40:40.204 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, I feel that life
is a lot more beautiful

00:40:40.204 --> 00:40:45.176
when you live that way, and it
makes evil putrid, you know?

00:40:46.644 --> 00:40:48.379
When you see how beautiful
most people are, really,

00:40:48.379 --> 00:40:49.880
and most relationships are.

00:40:49.880 --> 00:40:51.549
And then when somebody
comes along and says,

00:40:51.549 --> 00:40:54.251
"Oh, you should judge
that person by the
color of his skin."

00:40:54.251 --> 00:40:57.922
It is like a rank
smell. (laughs)

00:40:57.922 --> 00:40:59.423
And so we need to,

00:40:59.423 --> 00:41:01.058 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think conservatives
need to live a little bit.

00:41:01.058 --> 00:41:03.594
And you know what the
funny thing is, Dave?

00:41:03.594 --> 00:41:06.831
You know, as Spencer is engaged
to Josh, this wonderful guy,

00:41:06.831 --> 00:41:08.499 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he had a house party.
- Yup, yup.

00:41:08.499 --> 00:41:11.569 align:start size:84% position:16%
- So I go to the house
party and there's,

00:41:11.569 --> 00:41:13.504
you know, crazy gay
people coming in.

00:41:13.504 --> 00:41:14.972
There's, Josh's relatives

00:41:14.972 --> 00:41:17.208 align:start position:0%
were all these kind of down-home
Americans from the Midwest,

00:41:17.208 --> 00:41:19.310 align:start size:97% position:3%
and then there's all The Daily
Wire guys were all these,

00:41:19.310 --> 00:41:22.513 align:start size:84% position:16%
you know, kind of some
cigar-smoking guy,

00:41:22.513 --> 00:41:24.315 align:start size:88% position:13%
and they're all
right-wing Republicans.

00:41:24.315 --> 00:41:25.416
Every one of them-
- No, they can't.

00:41:25.416 --> 00:41:26.684 align:start size:84% position:16%
- is like a
right-wing Republican.

00:41:26.684 --> 00:41:28.986
And I just thought, "You
know, we live like this.

00:41:28.986 --> 00:41:30.521
Why shouldn't we
think like this?"

00:41:30.521 --> 00:41:32.122 align:start size:88% position:13%
They don't believe
what you don't believe.

00:41:32.122 --> 00:41:35.826 align:start size:94% position:6%
If we love, some of these
people who were there

00:41:35.826 --> 00:41:38.629 align:start size:94% position:6%
believe in a
philosophical conversation

00:41:38.629 --> 00:41:40.931
that gay marriage is the
end of Western civilization

00:41:40.931 --> 00:41:42.533
as we know it, but
they were there.

00:41:42.533 --> 00:41:43.767
Why? Because they love Spencer.

00:41:43.767 --> 00:41:44.735
They love Josh.

00:41:44.735 --> 00:41:46.303
They want them to be happy.

00:41:46.303 --> 00:41:49.206
I think like, "Well, why not
make that your philosophy?"

00:41:49.206 --> 00:41:51.175
You know, why not make the love,

00:41:51.175 --> 00:41:53.177 align:start size:94% position:6%
put the love at the
center of your philosophy

00:41:53.177 --> 00:41:56.447
and stop sitting around in
your head with ideas that,

00:41:56.447 --> 00:41:58.449 align:start size:88% position:13%
you know, may sound good
and may fit together,

00:41:58.449 --> 00:41:59.917 align:start size:84% position:16%
but actually, you're
not expressing

00:41:59.917 --> 00:42:01.051
that other part of yourself.

00:42:01.051 --> 00:42:02.219
- Well, that's the funny part

00:42:02.219 --> 00:42:03.754 align:start size:84% position:16%
because it's always
the anonymous

00:42:03.754 --> 00:42:05.422
Twitter and YouTube commentators

00:42:05.422 --> 00:42:08.425 align:start size:97% position:3%
telling me how much you
scary conservatives hate me.

00:42:08.425 --> 00:42:10.728 align:start size:88% position:13%
Meanwhile, we're
breaking bread together

00:42:10.728 --> 00:42:12.296 align:start size:97% position:3%
and having a hell of a time.
(Andrew laughs)

00:42:12.296 --> 00:42:15.199 align:start position:0%
And they're the ones in the
name of tolerance and diversity,

00:42:15.199 --> 00:42:16.967
telling me how much you hate me.

00:42:16.967 --> 00:42:18.168 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's quite extraordinary.
- It's amazing.

00:42:18.168 --> 00:42:19.904 align:start size:88% position:13%
- You kind of gotta
get a credit, you know?

00:42:19.904 --> 00:42:22.039
(Andrew laughs)

00:42:22.039 --> 00:42:24.108
- Yes, they're very
good at what they do.

00:42:24.108 --> 00:42:28.412
And to be fair, they own so
much communication territory.

00:42:28.412 --> 00:42:30.281
It's a little easier for
them than it is for us.

00:42:30.281 --> 00:42:32.750 align:start position:0%
We're always kind of struggling
to get our voices heard.

00:42:32.750 --> 00:42:35.886 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah, so as long as
we're talking about art,

00:42:35.886 --> 00:42:37.388
I've got your new book here.

00:42:37.388 --> 00:42:40.424
And, you know, people in our
world, in the political world,

00:42:40.424 --> 00:42:41.926
I don't think everyone knows

00:42:41.926 --> 00:42:43.961
that you've been a novelist
for decades at this point.

00:42:43.961 --> 00:42:44.895
(Andrew laughs)

00:42:44.895 --> 00:42:46.830
How many books have you written?

00:42:46.830 --> 00:42:48.299
- It's over 30.

00:42:48.299 --> 00:42:50.067
Yeah, I've lost track.

00:42:50.067 --> 00:42:52.603
- I mean, that's, as someone
that just finished my second,

00:42:52.603 --> 00:42:54.171
I don't know how you've
possibly written 30.

00:42:54.171 --> 00:42:55.239 align:start size:84% position:16%
(Andrew laughs)
It's pretty extreme.

00:42:55.239 --> 00:42:56.907
And you're writing novels also.

00:42:56.907 --> 00:42:57.875 align:start size:75% position:25%
So I mean like-
- Yes.

00:42:57.875 --> 00:42:59.944
- this is heavy-duty stuff.

00:42:59.944 --> 00:43:02.279
You write a lot of mysteries.

00:43:02.279 --> 00:43:04.648 align:start size:94% position:6%
How did you get
interested in all of this?

00:43:04.648 --> 00:43:06.183
And to some of the stuff,

00:43:06.183 --> 00:43:08.719
that stuff that you
bring to the novels,

00:43:08.719 --> 00:43:10.988
does that move into the
way you approach politics

00:43:10.988 --> 00:43:12.623
and culture and all that?

00:43:12.623 --> 00:43:15.225 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- Yeah, I mean, a
novel comes out of you.

00:43:15.225 --> 00:43:16.126 line:0%
It's a vision, you know?

00:43:16.126 --> 00:43:18.596 line:0%
And I loved,

00:43:18.596 --> 00:43:20.164 line:0%
you know, when I was a kid,

00:43:20.164 --> 00:43:21.966
I didn't really have
any male role models

00:43:21.966 --> 00:43:24.268 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I was desperate
to find them.

00:43:24.268 --> 00:43:25.703
And I found them in writers

00:43:25.703 --> 00:43:27.271
and most of them were
tough guy writers,

00:43:27.271 --> 00:43:29.006
guys like Ernest Hemingway.

00:43:29.006 --> 00:43:32.109
But essentially, the
ones I really loved

00:43:32.109 --> 00:43:33.944 align:start size:94% position:6%
were the mystery, the
tough guy mystery writer.

00:43:33.944 --> 00:43:36.847
So Dashiell Hammett, and
especially Raymond Chandler

00:43:36.847 --> 00:43:40.584 align:start size:84% position:16%
with his kind of
knight-like hero who,

00:43:40.584 --> 00:43:42.686
he sat down these mean streets,

00:43:42.686 --> 00:43:45.222 align:start size:84% position:16%
a man must go who
is not himself mean.

00:43:45.222 --> 00:43:47.257 align:start position:0%
And that's where we get the
phrase, down these mean streets.

00:43:47.257 --> 00:43:50.427
And I just loved that idea
that the world was corrupt,

00:43:50.427 --> 00:43:52.630 align:start size:94% position:6%
but you were gonna walk
through it just like this.

00:43:52.630 --> 00:43:54.164 align:start size:88% position:13%
You know, just with
your eyes on the right.

00:43:54.164 --> 00:43:55.633 align:start size:81% position:19%
And you were gonna
do that right.

00:43:55.633 --> 00:43:57.635 align:start size:97% position:3%
As a little kid, I just
thought that's who I wanna be.

00:43:57.635 --> 00:43:59.770
And so those were the books
that kind of inspired me.

00:43:59.770 --> 00:44:01.271
And as I became a writer,

00:44:01.271 --> 00:44:05.275
I kind of lost the track for
a while in my troubled youth.

00:44:05.275 --> 00:44:07.444 align:start size:88% position:13%
But when I straightened
my head out,

00:44:07.444 --> 00:44:08.946
I kind of came back to that

00:44:08.946 --> 00:44:13.651
and I consider myself as good
as any mystery writer alive,

00:44:14.785 --> 00:44:17.721 align:start size:97% position:3%
and if I may plug the book-
- Yeah.

00:44:17.721 --> 00:44:19.790 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Buy this book.
- We'll both hold the book

00:44:19.790 --> 00:44:21.592 align:start size:88% position:13%
at the exact same time.
- Yes. Yes.

00:44:21.592 --> 00:44:25.295
Because I will tell you, I
had this book in my mind for,

00:44:25.295 --> 00:44:28.899
I have the last scene of this
book in my mind for 25 years.

00:44:28.899 --> 00:44:30.901 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Wow.
- And I could not

00:44:30.901 --> 00:44:33.504 align:start size:84% position:16%
build the story to get
to that last scene.

00:44:33.504 --> 00:44:35.506
And I love Christmas,

00:44:35.506 --> 00:44:37.541
and I always wanted to
write a Christmas story,

00:44:37.541 --> 00:44:39.009
and I always thought

00:44:39.009 --> 00:44:40.544 align:start size:94% position:6%
the problem with most
Christmas stories are bad.

00:44:40.544 --> 00:44:42.079
You know, really only
"A Christmas Carol"

00:44:42.079 --> 00:44:44.014
and it is a wonderful life
or good Christmas stories.

00:44:44.014 --> 00:44:46.050 align:start size:84% position:16%
And the reason most
of them are bad

00:44:46.050 --> 00:44:48.452 align:start size:97% position:3%
is they're about you finding
something that you want.

00:44:48.452 --> 00:44:50.754
Finding love, or finding a home,

00:44:50.754 --> 00:44:52.990
or finding mom and
dad or whatever.

00:44:52.990 --> 00:44:56.427 align:start position:0%
But Christmas is actually about
sacrifice and about change.

00:44:56.427 --> 00:44:59.430 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's about what the
Bible, they call metanoia,

00:44:59.430 --> 00:45:02.566 align:start size:94% position:6%
which is turning around
and giving something away.

00:45:02.566 --> 00:45:04.168 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, giving something
that you love away

00:45:04.168 --> 00:45:05.402
in order to be free.

00:45:05.402 --> 00:45:07.137
And so I wanted to
write that story

00:45:07.137 --> 00:45:10.340
and I had the scene in my mind,
and I couldn't build a story

00:45:10.340 --> 00:45:12.276
that got honestly to that scene.

00:45:12.276 --> 00:45:14.678 align:start size:94% position:6%
So my friend Otto
Penzler who is the great,

00:45:16.513 --> 00:45:19.483 align:start size:88% position:13%
he is the greatest
American mystery editor.

00:45:19.483 --> 00:45:20.951
And we were in the pandemic

00:45:20.951 --> 00:45:23.253
and he was locked down
in the middle of nowhere.

00:45:23.253 --> 00:45:24.988
He's like down in Connecticut,

00:45:24.988 --> 00:45:27.291 align:start position:0%
and he called me up and we were
having these conversations,

00:45:27.291 --> 00:45:28.892
you know, I was kind
of keeping him company.

00:45:28.892 --> 00:45:33.263 align:start size:97% position:3%
And he said, "When are you
gonna write a Christmas book?"

00:45:33.263 --> 00:45:34.565
And I said, "Yeah."

00:45:34.565 --> 00:45:36.066
I said, "Would you publish it?"

00:45:36.066 --> 00:45:38.535
And he said, "Oh yeah, I'll
publish a Christmas book."

00:45:38.535 --> 00:45:41.739
So I thought I gotta
figure this book out.

00:45:41.739 --> 00:45:43.574
And I took two long walks.

00:45:43.574 --> 00:45:46.143 align:start position:0%
And at the end of the long
walk, this little bell went off,

00:45:46.143 --> 00:45:48.912 align:start size:84% position:16%
and I thought,
"That's the story."

00:45:48.912 --> 00:45:51.448 align:start size:97% position:3%
And it came out, I'm so happy
with the way it came out.

00:45:51.448 --> 00:45:55.018 align:start size:84% position:16%
It's been, it was
number 12 on Audible,

00:45:55.018 --> 00:45:57.521
and it was number 30 on Amazon.

00:45:57.521 --> 00:45:59.990
And the reviews have
just been ecstatic.

00:45:59.990 --> 00:46:02.493 align:start size:84% position:16%
I mean, people are
just like on the site,

00:46:02.493 --> 00:46:05.429 align:start size:94% position:6%
not people I know, they're
just raving about it.

00:46:05.429 --> 00:46:06.997
And I have to say.

00:46:06.997 --> 00:46:08.665 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't wanna oversell it,
but I have to say this.

00:46:08.665 --> 00:46:09.600
(Dave laughs)

00:46:09.600 --> 00:46:12.603
We need, we need quality art-

00:46:12.603 --> 00:46:14.538
- Yes, we need stories.
- On our side, you know?

00:46:14.538 --> 00:46:15.739
- Yeah.
- Yes.

00:46:15.739 --> 00:46:18.542
Because we have so
much right-wing can't

00:46:18.542 --> 00:46:23.547 align:start size:97% position:3%
and so much kind of foundering
political propaganda

00:46:24.715 --> 00:46:26.850 align:start size:84% position:16%
and not enough, like
just good stories

00:46:26.850 --> 00:46:29.987 align:start size:84% position:16%
that are imbued with
our view of life,

00:46:29.987 --> 00:46:31.188
which is the kind
of view of life

00:46:31.188 --> 00:46:32.122 align:start size:88% position:13%
that you and I have
just been talking about

00:46:32.122 --> 00:46:34.124
through this whole interview.

00:46:34.124 --> 00:46:36.794
And I will stand by this book.

00:46:36.794 --> 00:46:39.563
I do not believe anyone is
gonna read this book and say,

00:46:39.563 --> 00:46:40.764
"Wow, I got ripped off."

00:46:40.764 --> 00:46:42.266 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think you're
gonna read this book

00:46:42.266 --> 00:46:44.601
and it just is a really
good Christmas story.

00:46:44.601 --> 00:46:46.069
- Well, here's my
commitment to you.

00:46:46.069 --> 00:46:49.006 align:start size:97% position:3%
I only got the book yesterday,
so I have not read it yet,

00:46:49.006 --> 00:46:51.074
but I'm hopping on
a plane tomorrow,

00:46:51.074 --> 00:46:53.076
tomorrow, two days
to go to Nashville,

00:46:53.076 --> 00:46:54.444
and I'm gonna have
dinner with your son,

00:46:54.444 --> 00:46:55.512
and I do most of my reading

00:46:55.512 --> 00:46:56.446
on the plane-
- Excellent.

00:46:56.446 --> 00:46:58.649
- so I will start the book.

00:46:58.649 --> 00:47:00.884
I'm curious, you know, as
someone that's only written

00:47:00.884 --> 00:47:04.288
or has only written
two nonfiction books,

00:47:04.288 --> 00:47:05.789
when you're writing a mystery,

00:47:05.789 --> 00:47:08.425
do you have a freaking big
whiteboard in front of you,

00:47:08.425 --> 00:47:11.094
really plotting all
the twists and turns?

00:47:11.094 --> 00:47:12.696 align:start size:81% position:19%
Or are you just
kind of figure it,

00:47:12.696 --> 00:47:13.931 align:start size:88% position:13%
you like know where
you're going at the end

00:47:13.931 --> 00:47:16.700
and you just figure
it out as you go?

00:47:16.700 --> 00:47:20.304
- No, there are some
writers who actually,

00:47:20.304 --> 00:47:21.538
Ruth Rendell was one of them

00:47:21.538 --> 00:47:23.207
who actually right off
the top of their heads.

00:47:23.207 --> 00:47:24.675
I outline everything.

00:47:24.675 --> 00:47:26.577
And sometimes the outline
changes along the way.

00:47:26.577 --> 00:47:29.713 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know, when I see
that a character won't do

00:47:29.713 --> 00:47:31.215
what I wanted him to do,

00:47:31.215 --> 00:47:33.383
I have to change the plot to
accommodate the character.

00:47:33.383 --> 00:47:36.153
But if I know where I'm going,

00:47:36.153 --> 00:47:37.621
then I get the joy of writing.

00:47:37.621 --> 00:47:39.790
I hate outlining by the way,
and it takes me weeks to do it,

00:47:39.790 --> 00:47:41.491
and it's just dull.

00:47:41.491 --> 00:47:44.094
It's the only part of my
job I really find dull,

00:47:44.094 --> 00:47:47.197 align:start size:97% position:3%
but then I'm free to just
write the book and create it,

00:47:47.197 --> 00:47:48.665 align:start size:84% position:16%
and that's what I
really wanna be doing.

00:47:48.665 --> 00:47:50.267
So it's much easier that way.

00:47:50.267 --> 00:47:51.802
It always amazed me,

00:47:51.802 --> 00:47:53.303
but one of the thing about
writers who don't outline,

00:47:53.303 --> 00:47:55.539
is they tend to write the
same two or three books

00:47:55.539 --> 00:47:56.807
over and over again.

00:47:56.807 --> 00:47:58.909
And Ruth Rendell is
a wonderful writer,

00:47:58.909 --> 00:48:01.011 align:start size:97% position:3%
but after you've read, I
don't know, five of her books,

00:48:01.011 --> 00:48:03.013
you've kind of read
her books, and like,

00:48:04.147 --> 00:48:06.016
I don't mean to knock
her 'cause she is,

00:48:06.016 --> 00:48:07.184
some of her books
are just wonderful,

00:48:07.184 --> 00:48:08.986 align:start size:84% position:16%
especially her
Barbara Vine books,

00:48:08.986 --> 00:48:11.321 align:start size:84% position:16%
but I find if I
outline, I can say,

00:48:11.321 --> 00:48:12.923
"Oh, I've done this before,"

00:48:12.923 --> 00:48:15.225
and throw it away and
do something fresh
and new, you know?

00:48:15.225 --> 00:48:16.660
And that's really
been helpful to me

00:48:16.660 --> 00:48:18.762
and I like doing it that way.

00:48:18.762 --> 00:48:20.631
If you read this, if you
do read it on the plane,

00:48:20.631 --> 00:48:21.798
you know, I trust
you, I believe you.

00:48:21.798 --> 00:48:23.233 align:start size:88% position:13%
(Dave drowns out Andrew)
- I will, I will.

00:48:23.233 --> 00:48:26.436 align:start position:0%
- But I hope you will say
something online if you like it.

00:48:26.436 --> 00:48:27.704
(Dave laughs)

00:48:27.704 --> 00:48:28.639 align:start size:75% position:25%
- I will.
(Andrew laughs)

00:48:28.639 --> 00:48:30.374
- I promise you, you will get,

00:48:30.374 --> 00:48:34.011
if it's good, you will get the
Dave Rubin comment somewhere-

00:48:34.011 --> 00:48:35.679 align:start size:94% position:6%
- 'Cause for some reason-
- on Amazon.

00:48:35.679 --> 00:48:38.348 align:start size:97% position:3%
- for some reason, people
believe what you say. (laughs)

00:48:38.348 --> 00:48:40.584
- I'm wondering about it myself.

00:48:42.052 --> 00:48:44.221
Listen, before I let you go,
anything else on your mind?

00:48:44.221 --> 00:48:45.422
I kind of didn't wanna do

00:48:45.422 --> 00:48:46.590 align:start size:84% position:16%
too much of the
day-to-day politics.

00:48:46.590 --> 00:48:47.524 align:start size:75% position:25%
This is exactly-
- Yeah.

00:48:47.524 --> 00:48:49.059
- what I wanted to do with you,

00:48:49.059 --> 00:48:51.762
because I feel like everyone's
just going nuts with politics

00:48:51.762 --> 00:48:53.230 align:start size:97% position:3%
and COVID and everything else.
- Yup.

00:48:53.230 --> 00:48:54.998 align:start position:0%
- Is there anything else that's
just sort of been thinking,

00:48:54.998 --> 00:48:57.234
you know, you moved,
life seems pretty good,

00:48:57.234 --> 00:48:59.937
you got a new book out, you
seem happy, anything else?

00:49:01.238 --> 00:49:03.507
- It's a wonderful time for me,

00:49:03.507 --> 00:49:05.842
and I'm very grateful
to have come this far

00:49:05.842 --> 00:49:07.444 align:start size:84% position:16%
and actually be
doing what I'm doing

00:49:07.444 --> 00:49:09.313
and loving the people around me

00:49:09.313 --> 00:49:11.114 align:start size:94% position:6%
and loving the things
that I'm doing is really,

00:49:11.114 --> 00:49:12.316
it really is great.

00:49:12.316 --> 00:49:14.318
And yeah, the only
thing I would say is,

00:49:14.318 --> 00:49:16.186
I would just reiterate what
I said before, you know?

00:49:16.186 --> 00:49:20.090
That like we must,
we cannot be afraid.

00:49:20.090 --> 00:49:21.692
Almost everything the left does

00:49:21.692 --> 00:49:23.493
is geared toward
making us afraid.

00:49:23.493 --> 00:49:26.129
You know, wear a mask, get
a vaccine, wear two masks,

00:49:26.129 --> 00:49:27.631
wear six masks, you know?

00:49:27.631 --> 00:49:30.067
Put a mask on your children,
give your children a vaccine.

00:49:30.067 --> 00:49:31.902 align:start size:84% position:16%
Everything is
about being afraid.

00:49:31.902 --> 00:49:35.105 align:start size:97% position:3%
And not only that, but
they've made fear a virtue.

00:49:35.105 --> 00:49:37.808
What do you mean you're not
afraid of climate change?

00:49:37.808 --> 00:49:39.676 align:start size:84% position:16%
Are you a denier?
You must be a denier.

00:49:39.676 --> 00:49:41.979 align:start size:84% position:16%
You know, everything
is about fear.

00:49:41.979 --> 00:49:43.513
And the reason for that,

00:49:43.513 --> 00:49:45.182
you know, C.S. Lewis, the
great Christian apologist,

00:49:45.182 --> 00:49:46.683
he wrote about this.

00:49:46.683 --> 00:49:49.987
He said that the devil wants
you to be afraid because fear,

00:49:49.987 --> 00:49:52.656 align:start size:94% position:6%
courage is the
centerpiece of all virtue.

00:49:52.656 --> 00:49:55.025 align:start size:84% position:16%
You cannot be virtuous
without courage.

00:49:55.025 --> 00:49:56.393
And this is a moment,

00:49:56.393 --> 00:49:58.996 align:start size:88% position:13%
I won't lie, this is
one of the worst moments

00:49:58.996 --> 00:50:01.131
in American culture
I have ever seen.

00:50:01.131 --> 00:50:02.566
Our art stinks,
our music stinks,

00:50:02.566 --> 00:50:04.501
our movie stinks, our TV stinks,

00:50:04.501 --> 00:50:07.971
most, except for mine, many
of our novel stink. (laughs)

00:50:07.971 --> 00:50:09.806
But, and I think
the reason for that

00:50:09.806 --> 00:50:11.641
is we have gutted
our belief system

00:50:11.641 --> 00:50:15.445
of everything worthwhile, and
so people can't create art.

00:50:15.445 --> 00:50:18.281
If we are not afraid,

00:50:18.281 --> 00:50:20.450 align:start size:88% position:13%
we will pass through
this into a better time.

00:50:20.450 --> 00:50:22.119
There is simply no question.

00:50:22.119 --> 00:50:23.587 align:start size:84% position:16%
If we're afraid,
and if we surrender,

00:50:23.587 --> 00:50:27.657
and if we say yes or no,
okay, I won't say anything.

00:50:27.657 --> 00:50:29.326
Please don't call me racist.

00:50:29.326 --> 00:50:30.494
If we keep apologizing,

00:50:30.494 --> 00:50:32.729 align:start size:84% position:16%
like so much of
these canceled fools,

00:50:32.729 --> 00:50:34.331 align:start size:88% position:13%
they cancel you for
something and they say,

00:50:34.331 --> 00:50:36.500 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Oh, I'm so sorry, I
wanna be a better person."

00:50:36.500 --> 00:50:38.201
And then they just
devour you, you know?

00:50:38.201 --> 00:50:39.603
It's like blood in the water.

00:50:39.603 --> 00:50:41.872
I am actually very hopeful.

00:50:41.872 --> 00:50:43.907
I think we have come to
the end of something.

00:50:43.907 --> 00:50:45.942
And it's the end of
the post-war era,

00:50:45.942 --> 00:50:47.744
it's the end of
a post-war order.

00:50:47.744 --> 00:50:49.279
We need new ideas,

00:50:49.279 --> 00:50:50.881 align:start size:97% position:3%
and I think there are people
out there who are coming,

00:50:50.881 --> 00:50:53.683 align:start size:97% position:3%
Spencer, one of them coming
along with new ideas,

00:50:53.683 --> 00:50:55.085 align:start size:84% position:16%
new ways of thinking
about things.

00:50:55.085 --> 00:50:57.120
And I'm actually very hopeful,

00:50:57.120 --> 00:50:59.656 align:start size:84% position:16%
but we have to start
with the courage

00:50:59.656 --> 00:51:02.459 align:start position:0%
of all those people I was
talking about, who are central.

00:51:02.459 --> 00:51:03.660
They have to have courage.

00:51:03.660 --> 00:51:05.629
It can't just be
the outline heroes,

00:51:05.629 --> 00:51:07.330
it's gotta be the people at
the center who starts to say,

00:51:07.330 --> 00:51:09.332
"You know what? We're
gonna speak the truth.

00:51:09.332 --> 00:51:12.269
Even if you torment us."

00:51:12.269 --> 00:51:14.471
- Klavan you finish an interview

00:51:14.471 --> 00:51:16.606
like you know where it's going,

00:51:16.606 --> 00:51:19.076
just like a fine novelist would.

00:51:19.076 --> 00:51:22.145
I look forward to
you having whiskey

00:51:22.145 --> 00:51:26.583
and me having tequila in
person, one of these days,

00:51:26.583 --> 00:51:28.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
probably not in this town.
- You got it.

00:51:28.452 --> 00:51:29.953
(Andrew laughs)

00:51:29.953 --> 00:51:32.789
You gotta get out of there
and I can't wait to see you.

00:51:32.789 --> 00:51:34.424
I always, one thing I
hate about the media

00:51:34.424 --> 00:51:36.793
is I only see my
friends on camera.

00:51:36.793 --> 00:51:38.028
(Andrew laughs)

00:51:38.028 --> 00:51:39.996
- The book is "When
Christmas Comes."

00:51:39.996 --> 00:51:41.231
I'm gonna read it.

00:51:41.231 --> 00:51:42.132 align:start size:81% position:19%
And I, you know
what I'm gonna do?

00:51:42.132 --> 00:51:43.667
I'm gonna read it.

00:51:43.667 --> 00:51:46.036
And whether I love it or hate
it, or somewhere in between,

00:51:46.036 --> 00:51:49.139 align:start size:94% position:6%
I will send you my
comment before I post it,

00:51:49.139 --> 00:51:50.006 align:start size:84% position:16%
so that you can make-
- All right.

00:51:50.006 --> 00:51:51.274
- your argument.

00:51:51.274 --> 00:51:53.710
If I don't like it,
you can push back.

00:51:53.710 --> 00:51:55.011
If I like it too much,

00:51:55.011 --> 00:51:56.379
I know you wouldn't
want people to think

00:51:56.379 --> 00:51:58.548
that I'm like doing
you a favor here,

00:51:58.548 --> 00:52:00.250
so we'll figure it out.

00:52:00.250 --> 00:52:01.284
(Andrew laughs)

00:52:01.284 --> 00:52:03.487
- I know you'll tell the truth.

00:52:03.487 --> 00:52:05.055
- Good seeing you, my friend.

